there's no doubt about it sometimes you  go back to an old game you used to love  start playing and realize that maybe  this wasn't as good as you thought it  was nostalgia is a powerful thing as it  happens however 2002's Resident Evil  remake holds up really well and Resident  Evil HD is here to prove it in fact this  HD remaster of a remake of a horror  classic does a great job of looking like  you remember it looking the reason  that's important is because the way you  remember it is almost always better than  the way it really looked new textures  new lighting and other nifty tweaks  don't make Resident Evil HD look like a  modern game exactly but it does look  like an older game through modern eyes  the gameplay tweaks are less substantial  the most significant addition is a  control scheme that lets you use the  analog stick to move in the direction  you're pushing otherwise Resident Evil  HD reveals the same mystery and requires  you to solve the same puzzles as the  2002 version you play as either Jill  Valentine or Chris Redfield who are  members of a special ops force called  stars you spend most of the time  exploring a mansion in which you become  trapped while investigating the  disappearances of other stars members  though it's soon clear that the fate you  should most worry about is your own  even purely within a modern context this  is an engrossing adventure that benefits  from tense exploration and clever  environmental puzzles people often talk  about the scary bits but Resident Evil's  finest asset is how it invigorates  exploration behind every door are  glimpses at a mansion's history hints of  experiments gone wrong and bizarre  contraptions that border on the occult  solving environmental puzzles means  gaining access to creepier areas and  learning more about the mansion setting  you're trapped within don't get me wrong  though the freights remain these kinds  of horror story jump scares are a matter  of routine and modern games and cinema  but you'll probably still jump when  those zombie dogs crash through the  glass the fear is enhanced by how  helpless you are when an enemy lands an  attack the dogs get all the attention  but it's the shark swimming around in  aquatic arena that always most terrified  me and man entering the aquatic circle  still gets me tense  you don't freely control the camera  during your endeavors every viewing  angle enhances the claustrophobia while  allowing Capcom to carefully place props  and lights for maximum effects some of  the most dangerous places are also given  the most limited screen space which in  turn intensifies that fight-or-flight  instinct Resident Evil was created with  a clear eye for shot composition which  makes exploration all the more tense  fixed camera can be a source of  frustration however it can be annoying  for instance to have the camera change  angle every few seconds because it means  having to quickly adjust to how that  changes character movement that's the  Resident Evil conundrum mechanics that  keep you nervous and cautious can also  bog things down there's that limited  inventory for instance it makes you  carefully consider what objects to keep  with you but it also leads to ridiculous  shuffling like going to your stash  putting in a puzzle object taking out an  increment going to the typewriter saving  your game going back to your stash  putting the ribbon back and  putting the thing back into your  inventory that kind of stuff doesn't  enhance the atmosphere so much as it  feels like it wastes time all the same  there's a beautiful simplicity to  Resident Evil HD it's a reminder that  the best mysteries don't need convoluted  stories to be enthralling later Resident  Evil games would add more viruses and  unnecessary subplots but the original  allows that menacing mansion to do most  of the talking in this series less is  more there's backstory to uncover but  the focus is on the here ends now it is  you the mansion and your imagination as  it happens that trio makes for a fine  adventure without the need for more  gameplay styles more outbreaks and more  guns  you
 

